Trigeminal Neuralgia
Neurological & Neurodegenerative
Unilateral shock-like facial pain, Triggered by innocuous stimuli, Absence of objective sensory loss

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-053
Reason for Visit: Unbearable, lightning-bolt facial pain preventing all oral intake and causing severe distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage recorded a patient suffering from agonizing, repetitive facial pain spasms triggered by swallowing or speaking, resulting in complete refusal of food and fluids for 24 hours. Urgent in-person evaluation is required to rule out acute temporal arteritis or severe dental abscess, and to administer potent, specialized neuropathic analgesia to restore safe hydration before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "If the wind touches my right cheek, a lightning bolt shoots through my jaw and teeth. I haven't drank a drop of water since yesterday because swallowing sets it off."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a hyperacute onset of excruciating, severe, shock-like, and distinctly lancinating facial pain. The pain is strictly limited to the right maxillary and mandibular branches of the trigeminal nerve, completely sparing the opposite side of the face. Pain paroxysms last distinctly from a fraction of a second to several seconds per excruciating attack. Severe attacks are distinctly triggered by completely innocuous mechanical stimuli such as lightly touching the face, chewing, talking, or brushing teeth. The patient confirms a complete, absolute absence of persistently severe background aching pain strictly between the completely separate severe attacks. There is a specific, highly localized distinct trigger zone directly on the right nasolabial fold that reliably precipitates a severe attack. The patient exhibits extreme, distinctly profound fear of precipitating an attack, resulting in severe, unintended dehydration due to an absolute refusal to eat or swallow saliva.
Functional Status: Fully independent, but currently immobilized by fear of triggering pain; drooling due to refusal to swallow.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of acute dehydration and acute kidney injury; profound psychological distress.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/85 mmHg (pain response) | HR: 90 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Highly anxious, sitting perfectly still, covering the right side of the face with a hand without touching the skin, actively drooling.
Physical Exam: Physical examination reveals spasmodic, visibly painful wincing of the right facial musculature directly accompanying the severe shock when the patient attempts to speak (tic douloureux). Neurological exam demonstrates a strict, undeniable absence of any objective clinical sensory deficits or distinct motor weakness upon formal testing of the trigeminal and facial nerves (tested very carefully outside the trigger zone). Corneal reflex is intact bilaterally. The diagnostic tetrad is fulfilled: unilateral shock-like facial pain, pain triggered by innocuous stimuli, and absence of objective sensory loss. Oral cavity examination (limited) reveals no gross dental decay, abscess, or intraoral swelling. Temporal arteries are non-tender and pulsatile.
Environmental Observations: A fan in the room has been turned off and covered with a towel to prevent air currents. Unused water glasses sit on the table.
Medication Reconciliation: Paracetamol and Ibuprofen providing zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Trigeminal Neuralgia - Status: Acute Severe Pain Crisis
Differentials:

Dental Abscess / Pulpitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The pain is electrical and brief rather than a constant, severe throbbing ache; no fever, no localized gingival swelling, and the presence of a cutaneous trigger zone (nasolabial fold).

Temporal Arteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain is shock-like and brief, not a constant dull headache; temporal arteries are normal to palpation; absence of visual changes or jaw claudication (the pain is a trigger, not an ischemic cramp).

Post-Herpetic Neuralgia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of constant background burning pain, absence of scarring/rash history, and the strictly paroxysmal, shock-like nature of the attacks.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is trapped in a state of catastrophic neuropathic pain resulting in a total inability to maintain oral hydration due to severe mechanical triggering. Delaying clinical assessment would subject the patient to further excruciating agony and rapidly lead to acute dehydration requiring intravenous fluids in an emergency department. Urgent after-hours deployment definitively ruled out destructive local pathology (abscess, arteritis) and allowed for the immediate, critical initiation of targeted membrane-stabilizing anticonvulsant therapy (Carbamazepine). This acute pharmacological intervention rapidly desensitized the hyperactive trigeminal nerve root, allowing the patient to safely swallow and rehydrate, directly fulfilling MBS urgency criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Carbamazepine 100mg orally STAT (dissolved in a tiny amount of water to minimize swallowing effort) and 100mg BD ongoing.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Step 1: By strictly using a straw to drink lukewarm water placed on the unaffected (left) side of the mouth, the patient actively bypasses the hyper-sensitive trigger zones, immediately guaranteeing safe and painless hydration.
Step 2: By avoiding all hard, crunchy, or excessively hot/cold foods, the patient removes severe mechanical and thermal stimulation to the teeth and jaw, directly preventing violent spikes of electrical pain.
Step 3: By taking the Carbamazepine exactly on schedule before the pain returns, the patient proactively builds a chemical shield around the angry nerve, completely stopping the terrifying shocks before they can fire.
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 3 days to assess hydration status and titrate Carbamazepine dosing, followed by a non-urgent MRI brain to assess for vascular compression loops.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the Emergency Department if the medication causes a severe, widespread blistering skin rash (risk of Stevens-Johnson Syndrome), or if you remain completely unable to swallow any fluids for another 12 hours.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Initial medication may cause significant drowsiness or dizziness as the body adjusts.
Clinical Course: Carbamazepine is highly effective for this specific condition. The severe electrical shocks should dramatically reduce in frequency and intensity within 12-24 hours, allowing you to eat and drink normally again.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return for review if you develop a sudden high fever, sore throat, or unusual bleeding, as the new medication can rarely affect blood counts.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A blood vessel in your brain is likely rubbing against the main sensation nerve of your face, stripping its insulation. This makes the nerve hyperactive, so a light breeze feels like a lightning strike. Standard painkillers are completely useless here. We are using a specific seizure medication that acts as a powerful chemical brake on that nerve, stopping the violent electrical misfiring so you can swallow safely.
Actionable Steps: Take the Carbamazepine immediately. Use a straw on the left side of your mouth to drink water. Avoid chewing on the right side.

